Phase 3 — Governance & Reporting
Purpose

Phase 3 Governance exists to control execution, not decorate it.
This document defines who owns what, who can change what, how truth is reported, and how failure is handled.

If governance is vague, execution will rot.

1️⃣ Governance Objectives

Phase 3 governance must:

enforce alignment to Phase 0–2

prevent silent drift

ensure accountability exists

force honest reporting

create defensible decision trails

define when to continue and when to stop

If these aren’t happening, governance has failed.

2️⃣ Authority Structure

Execution authority is explicit, not implied.

Executive Sponsor

Responsible for:

program ownership

funding continuity

stop/go decisions when kill conditions trigger

defending execution integrity

May NOT:

override constraints for politics

authorize “just push through” when failure has occurred

Lead Engineer / Architecture Owner

Responsible for:

technical correctness

integrity of execution vs architectural intent

detecting drift and flagging it

recommending stop/go based on engineering reality

May NOT:

soften bad news

redefine success midstream

ignore risks called out in Phase 2

Independent Reviewer

Must be:

external to direct program control

not financially dependent on “success”

empowered to say “no”

Responsible for:

brutal honesty

checking alignment to original architecture

confirming integrity of reporting

certifying success or calling failure

If Independent Review is a rubber stamp, governance is fake.

3️⃣ Change Control

No undocumented changes. Ever.

Any change that affects:

purpose

architecture

scope

operating assumptions

risk posture

constraints

performance expectations

MUST:

be documented

be justified

receive tri-party approval:

Executive Sponsor

Lead Engineer

Independent Reviewer

If it doesn’t go through this, it does not happen.

4️⃣ Reporting Discipline

Reporting exists to describe reality, not protect egos.

Required Reports

Weekly Engineering Status

progress vs plan

failures + lessons

current risks

drift alerts

alignment statement (“still aligned / drift present”)

Monthly Governance Review

technical reality

institutional stability

cost vs expectation

risk outlook

license compliance confirmation

Event-Based Reports
Required immediately when:

severe failure occurs

drift is discovered

kill criteria approached

external pressure attempts to force bad decisions

Silence is failure.

5️⃣ Honesty Requirements

Reports must be:

complete

blunt

unfiltered

engineering first, politics second

No:

optimistic smoothing

“we think it’ll be fine” language

burying inconvenient truths

redefining words to avoid saying “failure”

Lies destroy programs faster than physics.

6️⃣ Transparency Rules

Engineers must be allowed to speak truth upward

Managers cannot suppress risk reporting

Executives do not get to demand positive outcomes

Independent Reviewer must have unfettered access

If people are afraid to speak, the program is already dead.

7️⃣ Governance Meetings

Minimum cadence:

Weekly Execution Sync

engineers + manager

keeps execution aligned

Biweekly Risk & Drift Review

engineering + independent reviewer

confirm: drift, risk, boundary integrity

Monthly Executive Governance Session

all three authorities present

stop/go validation

strategic positioning

documentation of decisions

Missing governance meetings = missing governance.

8️⃣ Kill Process

Kill is not political. It is procedural.

If kill conditions occur:

Lead Engineer documents and triggers Kill Notice

Independent Reviewer validates

Executive Sponsor signs final Stop order

Execution halts immediately. No “just push a little longer.”

Stopping is discipline. Continuing after failure is negligence.

9️⃣ Compliance with License

Governance must ensure:

execution stays within licensed rights

no unauthorized builds

no shadow testing

no “just experimenting quietly”

Violating the license is:

legal risk

integrity risk

reputational risk

Governance treats violations as program-threatening events.

🔟 Final Governance Integrity Check

Governance is only real if:

☐ roles are enforced

☐ reporting is honest

☐ authority boundaries are respected

☐ changes are controlled

☐ transparency exists

☐ drift gets corrected

☐ kills happen when required

If you need politics, fear, or spin to keep the program alive, it’s already failed.
